1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,860
He is credited with
352 aerial victories.

2
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:08,450
A Soviet court sentenced
him to 25 years in prison.

3
00:00:08,450 --> 00:00:10,520
And later he led the first jet

4
00:00:10,520 --> 00:00:12,760
fighter wing of the Bundeswehr.

5
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:14,180
Erich Hartmann.

6
00:00:14,180 --> 00:00:18,920
Erich Hartmann was born
in April 1922 in Weissach

7
00:00:18,920 --> 00:00:20,230
in Württemberg.

8
00:00:20,230 --> 00:00:24,710
His father was a doctor,
his mother Elisabeth a glider pilot.

9
00:00:24,710 --> 00:00:27,790
Because of the severe
economic situation,

10
00:00:27,790 --> 00:00:29,560
the family moved to China,

11
00:00:29,560 --> 00:00:34,230
where Erich grew up, until civil
war broke out there in 1928 and they

12
00:00:34,230 --> 00:00:35,780
returned to Germany.

13
00:00:35,780 --> 00:00:38,230
His mother taught him to fly,

14
00:00:38,230 --> 00:00:41,870
and at 14 he was already
giving gliding lessons himself.

15
00:00:41,870 --> 00:00:45,430
After finishing school,
he joined the Luftwaffe in

16
00:00:45,430 --> 00:00:46,810
October 1940.

17
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:50,790
His training as a fighter
pilot took almost two years and

18
00:00:50,790 --> 00:00:52,800
began with a setback.

19
00:00:52,800 --> 00:00:58,420
In March 1942 he flew forbidden
aerobatics over an airfield

20
00:00:58,420 --> 00:01:00,520
and was punished.

21
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:04,220
A comrade took over his replacement
flight and was killed in

22
00:01:04,220 --> 00:01:05,600
a crash landing.

23
00:01:05,600 --> 00:01:10,960
In autumn 1942 Hartmann
joined Fighter Wing 52 on the

24
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:12,030
Eastern Front.

25
00:01:12,030 --> 00:01:16,220
His very first mission on 14 October
ended in an emergency landing,

26
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:19,410
because he had lost
his flight leader

27
00:01:19,410 --> 00:01:23,100
and ran out of fuel before
reaching the airfield.

28
00:01:23,100 --> 00:01:26,100
The punishment was three
days of ground duty.

29
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:29,900
After that, Flight Sergeant
Edmund Roßmann became his teacher.

30
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:30,940
He taught him

31
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:34,940
to keep his distance and to
fire only from close range.

32
00:01:34,940 --> 00:01:39,060
Hartmann's principle later was:
"Fly with your head,

33
00:01:39,060 --> 00:01:40,540
not with your muscles."

34
00:01:40,540 --> 00:01:45,490
In early November 1942 he
reported his first victory,

35
00:01:45,490 --> 00:01:49,120
an Ilyushin Il-2. Debris
damaged his aircraft, and he had

36
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:52,080
to make another
emergency landing.

37
00:01:52,080 --> 00:01:57,210
Until spring 1943 Hartmann
reported only a few victories.

38
00:01:57,210 --> 00:01:59,450
Because of his youthful looks,

39
00:01:59,450 --> 00:02:01,240
his comrades called him "Bubi".

40
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:04,140
The name is said to come
from Walter Krupinski,

41
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:07,540
who kept telling him over
the radio to get closer.

42
00:02:07,540 --> 00:02:09,680
In the summer his numbers
then rose quickly.

43
00:02:09,680 --> 00:02:12,570
On 7 July, during
the Battle of Kursk,

44
00:02:12,570 --> 00:02:16,570
he reported seven
victories in a single day.

45
00:02:16,570 --> 00:02:19,870
His method was: observe, decide,

46
00:02:19,870 --> 00:02:21,850
attack and break away.

47
00:02:21,850 --> 00:02:24,950
In August, by his own account,
he briefly fell into

48
00:02:24,950 --> 00:02:28,730
Soviet hands after an emergency
landing and managed to escape.

49
00:02:28,730 --> 00:02:33,700
On 2 September 1943 he took
command of the 9th squadron of

50
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:38,950
Fighter Wing 52. In total he had
to make 16 emergency landings.

51
00:02:38,950 --> 00:02:42,570
He was never shot down by an
opponent in aerial combat.

52
00:02:42,570 --> 00:02:49,080
On 29 October 1943, with 148
victories, he received the

53
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:50,440
Knight's Cross.

54
00:02:50,440 --> 00:02:53,820
One of his aircraft bore
a painted heart for his

55
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:56,770
future wife Ursula,
known as "Usch".

56
00:02:56,770 --> 00:03:02,600
In March 1944, at 202 victories,
the Oak Leaves followed,

57
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:05,920
and in July, at 268, the Swords.

58
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:12,220
On 24 August 1944 he reported eleven
victories in one day, passing the 300 mark.

59
00:03:12,220 --> 00:03:16,400
Researchers view this number
critically, because apparently

60
00:03:16,400 --> 00:03:19,620
the successes of
the whole group were

61
00:03:19,620 --> 00:03:21,170
credited to him.

62
00:03:21,170 --> 00:03:24,770
One day later,
with 301 victories,

63
00:03:24,770 --> 00:03:29,070
he received the Diamonds,
one of only 27 such awards.

64
00:03:29,070 --> 00:03:33,270
Two weeks later,
on 10 September 1944, he married

65
00:03:33,270 --> 00:03:35,420
Ursula Paetsch, whom he had

66
00:03:35,420 --> 00:03:37,170
known since school.

67
00:03:37,170 --> 00:03:40,150
He was offered a transfer
to the new jet fighters

68
00:03:40,150 --> 00:03:42,190
several times, and he declined.

69
00:03:42,190 --> 00:03:46,160
On 8 May 1945 he reported
his last victory,

70
00:03:46,160 --> 00:03:47,790
over Brünn.

71
00:03:47,790 --> 00:03:51,910
In total he had
flown 1,404 missions.

72
00:03:51,910 --> 00:03:55,260
His unit then destroyed its
aircraft and surrendered

73
00:03:55,260 --> 00:03:57,290
to American troops.

74
00:03:57,290 --> 00:04:02,720
On 14 May 1945 the Americans
handed Hartmann and his men over

75
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:04,520
to the Red Army.

76
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:07,640
He refused to cooperate
with the Soviets and,

77
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:11,420
by his own account,
was put in solitary confinement.

78
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:16,260
At the end of 1949 he was
sentenced to 20 years in

79
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:21,240
prison, and in 1951,
in a second trial, to 25 years.

80
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:23,170
Among other things,

81
00:04:23,170 --> 00:04:26,640
he was accused of
killing 780 civilians and

82
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:28,430
attacking a bakery.

83
00:04:28,430 --> 00:04:30,140
Hartmann denied this.

84
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:33,260
Today the trial is considered
politically motivated.

85
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:40,170
His son Erich Peter was born in
1945 and died in 1948. Hartmann

86
00:04:40,170 --> 00:04:41,840
never saw him.

87
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:45,990
In October 1955 he was
released with the last German

88
00:04:45,990 --> 00:04:47,540
prisoners of war.

89
00:04:47,540 --> 00:04:51,840
In 1997 the Russian military
prosecutor's office overturned

90
00:04:51,840 --> 00:04:53,170
the verdicts.

91
00:04:53,170 --> 00:05:00,240
In 1956 he joined the new Bundeswehr
and from 1957 was trained on

92
00:05:00,240 --> 00:05:03,210
jet aircraft in the USA.

93
00:05:03,210 --> 00:05:09,820
From 1959 to 1962 he commanded Fighter
Wing 71 "Richthofen", the first

94
00:05:09,820 --> 00:05:13,510
all-jet fighter unit
of the West German

95
00:05:13,510 --> 00:05:14,960
Air Force.

96
00:05:14,960 --> 00:05:18,890
He considered the Starfighter
unsuitable and said so openly.

97
00:05:18,890 --> 00:05:21,760
Later many of these
aircraft crashed,

98
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:24,690
and more than 100
German pilots died.

99
00:05:24,690 --> 00:05:28,980
In 1970 he was retired early,
probably partly because

100
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:31,010
of this criticism.

101
00:05:31,010 --> 00:05:34,990
After that he worked as a flight
instructor in Hangelar near Bonn.

102
00:05:34,990 --> 00:05:40,790
He died on 20 September 1993
at the age of 71 in Weil

103
00:05:40,790 --> 00:05:41,890
im Schönbuch.

104
00:05:41,890 --> 00:05:44,430
Hartmann's second
logbook was lost.

105
00:05:44,430 --> 00:05:48,500
His biographers therefore
compiled the later

106
00:05:48,500 --> 00:05:50,910
claims from other sources.

107
00:05:50,910 --> 00:05:53,520
Some of these claims cannot be

108
00:05:53,520 --> 00:05:56,190
reconciled with
Soviet loss records.

109
00:05:56,190 --> 00:06:00,330
In 2005 the Russian researcher
Dmitri Khazanov estimated

110
00:06:00,330 --> 00:06:04,220
the actual number at
70 to 80 victories.

111
00:06:04,220 --> 00:06:07,200
Most historians do not
follow this estimate.

112
00:06:07,200 --> 00:06:11,890
The 1970 book "The Blond Knight
of Germany" was criticized as

113
00:06:11,890 --> 00:06:15,030
one-sided by the
historians Ronald Smelser

114
00:06:15,030 --> 00:06:16,850
and Edward Davies.

115
00:06:16,850 --> 00:06:20,000
Hartmann was buried in
the New Cemetery in Weil

116
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:21,470
im Schönbuch.

117
00:06:21,470 --> 00:06:24,000
Did you know who was
really behind the name

118
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:25,420
Erich Hartmann?

119
00:06:25,420 --> 00:06:27,120
Write it in the comments!

